1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:01,400
Speaker 1: All right, let's begin.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,180
Speaker 1: We have 12 items on the agenda.

3
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:06,900
Speaker 1: I want to finish in 90 minutes.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,820
Speaker 1: First item, the curriculum revision for the AI and ML program.

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,380
Speaker 1: Jaishree, please present.

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:16,760
Speaker 0: Thank you, sir.

7
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:21,280
Speaker 0: So, the department has proposed three major changes.

8
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:27,440
Speaker 0: First, replacing the data mining elective with a new course called Applied Deep Learning.

9
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:33,540
Speaker 0: Second, Updating the NLP course syllabus to include large language models.

10
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:39,080
Speaker 0: Third, making machine learning a prerequisite for three advanced electives.

11
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:42,000
Speaker 3: Jaishree, hold on.

12
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,840
Speaker 3: Why are we removing data mining?

13
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,320
Speaker 3: It's a foundational course.

14
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:51,860
Speaker 0: Sir, the enrollment has dropped significantly.

15
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:57,900
Speaker 0: Last two years, only 11 and 14 students respectively opted for it.

16
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:00,960
Speaker 3: That doesn't mean we remove it.

17
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:03,240
Speaker 3: It means we improve it.

18
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:06,120
Speaker 2: Actually, if I may.

19
00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:10,360
Speaker 2: At our institution, we had the same issue with data mining.

20
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:13,740
Speaker 2: We merged it with the statistical learning course.

21
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:15,980
Speaker 2: That approach worked well.

22
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:18,760
Speaker 0: We considered that.

23
00:01:19,900 --> 00:01:23,800
Speaker 0: But we already have an advanced ML course covering similar ground.

24
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:26,560
Speaker 0: Adding another would be redundant.

25
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:29,140
Speaker 3: I'm not convinced.

26
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:34,180
Speaker 3: Data mining covers things like association rules, frequent pattern mining.

27
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:37,200
Speaker 3: Things not in standard ML courses.

28
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:40,560
Speaker 1: Let's table this specific point.

29
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:46,140
Speaker 1: Kumar, can you prepare a brief justification for keeping data mining by next meeting?

30
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:48,980
Speaker 3: Yes, I'll have it ready.

31
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:52,920
Speaker 1: Jaishree, continue with the other changes.

32
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:55,960
Speaker 0: The NLP update.

33
00:01:57,060 --> 00:02:04,160
Speaker 0: We want to include a module on transformer architectures, fine-tuning, and practical deployment of language models.

34
00:02:05,260 --> 00:02:09,620
Speaker 0: The current syllabus stops at word embeddings and basic sequence models.

35
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:12,640
Speaker 2: That's a reasonable update.

36
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,240
Speaker 2: Industry is moving fast here.

37
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:17,480
Speaker 2: What's the assessment like?

38
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:21,400
Speaker 0: 60% continuous evaluation.

39
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:23,460
Speaker 0: 40% end semester.

40
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:29,880
Speaker 0: We have a mandatory group project where students build and evaluate an NLP application.

41
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:35,020
Speaker 2: Good What about the computational requirements?

42
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:38,820
Speaker 2: Training transformers need significant resources.

43
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,080
Speaker 0: We're using pre-trained models and fine-tuning.

44
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:45,680
Speaker 0: Not training from scratch.

45
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:50,040
Speaker 0: The lab has enough GPUs for fine-tuning experiments.

46
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:53,280
Speaker 3: How many GPUs exactly?

47
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:55,620
Speaker 0: Currently four.

48
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:58,140
Speaker 0: We are procuring four more.

49
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:00,660
Speaker 3: With what budget?

50
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:05,000
Speaker 0: 2.2 lakhs approved for Phase 1.

51
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:08,120
Speaker 0: Phase 2 will be requested next quarter.

52
00:03:09,820 --> 00:03:10,380
Speaker 1: Moving on.

53
00:03:11,460 --> 00:03:13,440
Speaker 1: The third change, prerequisites.

54
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:21,800
Speaker 0: We want machine learning to be a mandatory prerequisite for deep learning, computer vision and reinforcement learning.

55
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:25,320
Speaker 0: Currently these are open to any student.

56
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:28,280
Speaker 2: I strongly support this.

57
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:34,120
Speaker 2: We've seen too many students struggling in advanced electives because they lack ML fundamentals.

58
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:37,000
Speaker 3: Agreed on deep learning.

59
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:38,880
Speaker 3: But computer vision?

60
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:42,300
Speaker 3: That's more linear algebra and image processing.

61
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:45,200
Speaker 3: ML prerequisite might be overkill.

62
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:51,660
Speaker 0: The modern computer vision curriculum is basically deep learning applied to images.

63
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:56,280
Speaker 0: CNNs, vision transformers, generative models.

64
00:03:57,400 --> 00:04:00,440
Speaker 0: Without ML background, students can't follow.

65
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:03,660
Speaker 3: Fine, I'll concede on that.

66
00:04:05,420 --> 00:04:07,080
Speaker 2: One question on implementation.

67
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:11,440
Speaker 2: What happens to students currently enrolled without the prerequisite?

68
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:14,060
Speaker 0: Grandfathered in.

69
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:18,839
Speaker 0: The prerequisite applies to batches starting next academic year.

70
00:04:20,579 --> 00:04:22,300
Speaker 1: All right, voting on the package.

71
00:04:23,380 --> 00:04:24,240
Speaker 1: Three changes.

72
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:29,200
Speaker 1: First, data mining replacement, deferred pending Qumars review.

73
00:04:30,420 --> 00:04:32,540
Speaker 1: Second, NLP syllabus update.

74
00:04:33,580 --> 00:04:36,580
Speaker 1: Third, prerequisite changes for advanced electives.

75
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:39,460
Speaker 1: All in favor of items 2 and 3.

76
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:41,840
Speaker 3: In favor.

77
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:44,120
Speaker 2: In favor.

78
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:46,420
Speaker 0: In favor.

79
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:48,680
Speaker 1: Approved.

80
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:53,560
Speaker 1: Jaishree, please update the curriculum document and circulate by Friday.

81
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:57,400
Speaker 1: Kumar, data mining decision goes to next meeting.

82
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:02,280
Speaker 1: Moving to item 2, the capstone project framework.

83
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:06,140
Speaker 0: We've revised the capstone timeline.

84
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:09,740
Speaker 0: Phase 1 is now 14 weeks instead of 12.

85
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:13,640
Speaker 0: Based on student feedback about workload.

86
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:18,980
Speaker 0: Assessment split remains 30-70 between phase 1 and phase 2.

87
00:05:18,980 --> 00:05:22,160
Speaker 3: 14 weeks for phase 1?

88
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,640
Speaker 3: That's more than the phase 2 timeline of 16 weeks.

89
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:27,780
Speaker 3: Doesn't seem balanced.

90
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:31,720
Speaker 0: Phase 2 is primarily implementation.

91
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:37,340
Speaker 0: Phase 1 is literature review, problem formulation, and prototype design.

92
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:41,060
Speaker 0: It genuinely takes longer when done properly.

93
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:44,760
Speaker 2: What's the literature survey requirement?

94
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:47,700
Speaker 0: Minimum 4 papers.

95
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:52,460
Speaker 0: Students must critically analyze, not just summarize.

96
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:55,920
Speaker 3: How do you grade critical analysis?

97
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:57,860
Speaker 3: That's objective.

98
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:00,440
Speaker 0: We have a rubric.

99
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:02,840
Speaker 0: 10 points for summary.

100
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:11,600
Speaker 0: 15 points for critical analysis, which includes identifying gaps, limitations, comparison across papers.

101
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:14,500
Speaker 0: Specific criteria for each.

102
00:06:16,240 --> 00:06:17,140
Speaker 2: Send me the rubric.

103
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:21,040
Speaker 2: I can review it for fairness across teams and graders.

104
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:24,100
Speaker 0: I'll email it this evening.

105
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:28,300
Speaker 1: The extension to 14 weeks, I approve.

106
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:32,380
Speaker 1: What about the mandatory peer review Chetna mentioned in her note?

107
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:34,400
Speaker 0: Yes.

108
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:38,560
Speaker 0: Each team presents their phase 1 to two other teams.

109
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:42,340
Speaker 0: Teams give structured feedback using a rubric.

110
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:46,880
Speaker 0: This happens in week 12 to weeks before submission.

111
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:49,340
Speaker 3: Is this graded?

112
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:51,280
Speaker 0: Yes.

113
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:57,800
Speaker 0: 5% of the phase 1 grade is based on the quality of feedback given to other teams.

114
00:06:58,160 --> 00:06:59,180
Speaker 0: Not just received.

115
00:07:00,860 --> 00:07:01,420
Speaker 2: I like this.

116
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:04,920
Speaker 2: It incentivizes engagement with peers' work.

117
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:07,200
Speaker 1: Approved.

118
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:09,600
Speaker 1: Anything else on Capstone?

119
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:12,060
Speaker 0: Not for now.

120
00:07:13,780 --> 00:07:14,200
Speaker 1: Item 3.

121
00:07:15,460 --> 00:07:17,360
Speaker 1: External industry collaborations.

122
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:20,020
Speaker 1: We received three proposals.

123
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:23,460
Speaker 1: One from Infosys for a joint research lab.

124
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,920
Speaker 1: One from Bosch for an embedded systems elective.

125
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:30,860
Speaker 1: One from NVIDIA for GPU computing workshops.

126
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:34,160
Speaker 3: Infosys is the most committed.

127
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,200
Speaker 3: They've done good work with other institutions.

128
00:07:38,980 --> 00:07:40,420
Speaker 2: I know the Bosch proposal well.

129
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:45,020
Speaker 2: It's genuinely good for students interested in automotive software.

130
00:07:46,100 --> 00:07:47,960
Speaker 2: Hands-on with real embedded hardware.

131
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:53,320
Speaker 0: NVIDIA workshops would complement our AI program well.

132
00:07:54,980 --> 00:07:56,300
Speaker 1: We're running low on time.

133
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:02,140
Speaker 1: Let me propose we approve exploring all three, but prioritize based on resources.

134
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:04,860
Speaker 1: Infosys first given scope.

135
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:08,500
Speaker 1: Then Nvidia Workshops since their shorter duration.

136
00:08:09,580 --> 00:08:12,560
Speaker 1: Bosch 3rd since embedded isn't our primary focus area.

137
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:14,860
Speaker 3: Fine with me.

138
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:17,040
Speaker 2: Agreed.

139
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:19,300
Speaker 0: Agreed.

140
00:08:21,100 --> 00:08:22,420
Speaker 1: Okay, that's approved.

141
00:08:23,460 --> 00:08:25,940
Speaker 1: We'll cover items 4 through 12 next meeting.

142
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:28,780
Speaker 1: Scheduled for February 28th.

143
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:31,920
Speaker 1: Thank you all.

